{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[G]Well, in 18 and 14, we [C]took a little trip
[D]Along with Col. Jackson down the [G]mighty Mississip'
We [G]took a little bacon & we [C]took a little beans
And we [D]met the bloddy British near the [G]town of New Orleans
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Chorus 1}
We [G]fired our guns & the British kept a-comin'
[G]There wasn't nigh as many as they [D]was a while [G]ago
We [G]fired once more and they began a run-ning
[G]Down the MIssissippi to the [D]Gulf of [G]Mexico
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[G]Well I see'd Marse Jackson come [C]a-walkin' down the street
And [D]a-talking to a pirate by the [G]name of Jean Lafitte
He [G]gave Jean a drink that he'd [C]brung from Tennessee
And the [D]pirate said he'd help us drive the [G]British to the sea
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[G]Well the French told Andrew [C]"You had better run
'For [D]Packenham's a-comin' with a [G]bullet in his gun"
Old [G]Hickory said he [C]didn't give a damn
He's [D]a-gonna  whup the britches off of Col. [G]Packenham
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[G]Well,we looked down the river & we [C]seed the British come
And there [D]must have been a hundred of 'em [G]beatin' on the drum
They [G]stepped so high and they [C]made their bugles ring
While we [D]stood beside our cotton bales & [G]didn't say a thing
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
Old [G]Hickory said we could [C]take em by surprise
If we [D]didn't fire a musket till we [G]looked 'em in the eyes
We [G]held our fire til we [C]seed their faces well
Then we [D]opened up our squirrel guns & [G]really gave 'em well..
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Chorus 2}
Well they [G]ran thru the briars & they ran thru the brambles
And they [G]ran thru the bushes where a [D]rabbit couldn't [G]go
They [G]ran so fast that the hounds couldn't catch 'em
[G]Down the Mississippi to the [D]Gulf of [G]Mexico
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
Well we [G]fired our cannons till the [C]barrels melted down
So we [D]grabbed an alligator and we [G]fought another round
We [G]filled his head with minie balls and [C]powdered his behind
And when we [D]touched the powder off, the [G]'gator lost his mind
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 7}
They [G]lost their pants & their [C]pretty shiny coats
And their [D]tails wer all a-showin' like a [G]bunch of billy goats
They [G]ran down the river til their [C]tongues were hangin' out
And they [D]said they got a lickin' which there [G]wasn't any doubt
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 8}
Well [G]we marched back to town in our [C]dirty ragged pants
And we [D]danced all night with them [G]pretty firls from France
We [G]couldn't understand 'em but they [C]had the sweetest charms
And we [D]understood 'em better when we [G]got 'em in our arms
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 9}
Well the [G]guide who brung the [C]British from the sea
Came [D]a-limpin' into camp just as [G]sick as he could be
He [G]said the dyin' words of [C]Col. Packenham
Was [D]"You'd better quit foolin' with your [G]cousin Uncle Sam'
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 10}
Well, we'll [G]march back home, but [C]we'll never be content
Till we [D]make Old Hick'ry the [G]people's president.
And [G]every time we think about the [C]bacon and the beans
We'll [D]think about the fun we had [G]way down in New Orleans.
{end_of_verse}
